 "grammar": [
  {
   "title": "-t means he, she or it",
   "rules": [
    "verb-3sg"
   ],
   "body": "*rīdet* means 'she laughs' or 'he laughs' (or 'it laughs') – also 'is laughing': Latin has one present tense for both. The ending **-t** says the subject is one person or thing.\n\nIf the sentence names the subject, that word is the subject: *Puella rīdet* 'The girl laughs.' With no subject word, *rīdet* alone means 'she / he laughs'.\n\nVerbs come in families. Today's are *-at* verbs (*labōrat*, *stat*) and *-et* verbs (*timet*, *manet*, *rīdet*). Each word card also shows the verb's dictionary form, the 'I …' form in -ō – you will use that form in lesson 10.\n\nThe verb usually comes **last**, and little words such as *nunc* and *saepe* sit before it: *Fēmina saepe labōrat* 'The woman often works.'",
